<h2 id="ten-easy-jobs">10 entry-level jobs in Syracuse you can apply for</h2>

<h3>1. Retail sales associate</h3>
<p><strong>What you do:</strong> help shoppers, run the register, restock shelves and keep the sales floor tidy. Larger stores may also have you process online pickup orders.</p>
<p><strong>Typical requirements:</strong> no experience, a friendly attitude and availability on evenings, weekends and holidays. Most stores train you in the first week.</p>
<p><strong>Where to look:</strong> large shopping centers such as Destiny USA, plus department stores, grocery chains, pharmacies and big-box stores around the suburbs. Hiring ramps up ahead of the holiday season.</p>

<h3>2. Food service worker</h3>
<p><strong>What you do:</strong> take orders, prep food, run food to tables, wash dishes or work the drive-thru. It's fast-paced, and you'll build multitasking skills quickly.</p>
<p><strong>Typical requirements:</strong> no experience for most crew, dishwasher and counter jobs. Servers and bartenders may need some experience, and tips can add up for those roles.</p>
<p><strong>Where to look:</strong> fast-food and fast-casual chains, coffee shops, local diners, hotel restaurants, and dining services at colleges and hospitals, which often offer steadier daytime hours.</p>

<h3>3. Delivery driver</h3>
<p><strong>What you do:</strong> pick up food, groceries or packages and deliver them to customers. With apps such as Uber Eats, DoorDash and Grubhub, you choose when to work.</p>
<p><strong>Typical requirements:</strong> a valid driver's license, an insured vehicle, a smartphone and a clean driving record. Some services accept bikes in dense areas.</p>
<p><strong>Where to look:</strong> delivery apps, local pizza shops and restaurants that employ their own drivers, and parcel or courier companies. Keep in mind that app drivers are usually independent contractors who pay their own gas and taxes, so track your miles.</p>

