<p><strong>What you do:</strong> answer calls, chats and emails, solve billing or order problems, and log each interaction in a computer system.</p>
<p><strong>Typical requirements:</strong> clear speaking and writing, basic typing and computer skills, and patience. Many employers provide paid training, and some roles become remote after training.</p>
<p><strong>Where to look:</strong> insurance companies, banks, utilities, healthcare billing offices and call centers in the region, plus national companies hiring remote reps in New York.</p>

<h3>5. Housekeeping and cleaning services</h3>
<p><strong>What you do:</strong> clean rooms, offices or homes: vacuuming, dusting, restocking supplies and sanitizing bathrooms. You'll often work independently once you know the routine.</p>
<p><strong>Typical requirements:</strong> no experience, physical stamina and reliability. Some commercial cleaning jobs run overnight, which can suit people with daytime commitments.</p>
<p><strong>Where to look:</strong> hotels, hospitals, universities, senior living communities, janitorial contractors and residential cleaning companies. You can also build your own client list by word of mouth.</p>

