<h3>8. Childcare provider</h3>
<p><strong>What you do:</strong> supervise kids, plan simple activities, help with homework and keep children safe, whether at a daycare, an after-school program or as a babysitter or nanny.</p>
<p><strong>Typical requirements:</strong> patience, reliability and a background check. Licensed programs in New York follow state rules on screening and training, and CPR and first aid certification will make you a stronger candidate.</p>
<p><strong>Where to look:</strong> daycare centers, after-school programs, summer camps, YMCAs, community centers and family-matching sites for babysitters and nannies.</p>

<h3>9. Receptionist</h3>
<p><strong>What you do:</strong> greet visitors, answer and route calls, check people in for appointments and handle light admin work. You're the first impression of the business.</p>
<p><strong>Typical requirements:</strong> a professional, friendly manner and basic computer skills. Most roles have regular weekday hours, which is a plus if you want a predictable schedule.</p>
<p><strong>Where to look:</strong> medical clinics, salons and spas, car dealerships, hotels, gyms, and corporate offices.</p>

<h3>10. Security guard</h3>
<p><strong>What you do:</strong> monitor buildings and events, control access, make patrol rounds and write incident reports.</p>
